WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.303 --> 00:00:10.308
Continuity answers one question for an exchange, custodian or payments firm: was this deposit credited to the customer exactly once?

2
00:00:11.314 --> 00:00:16.905
It reads the payment from two Mainnet servers and compares it with the credits your own books posted.

3
00:01:39.627 --> 00:01:45.858
Here is a real issued-token payment read from Mainnet a moment ago, and one posted credit for it.

4
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:51.717
In production, that credit comes straight from your accounting database through the read-only adapter.

5
00:01:52.721 --> 00:01:58.739
The transaction hash goes here. Press Verify, and both servers are asked for the same payment.

6
00:02:01.248 --> 00:02:10.551
Both servers agree. Every check is listed: validated, destination, issuer, currency, delivered amount, and exactly one matching credit.

7
00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:16.894
The report keeps the ledger index and hash, so an auditor can repeat the reading.

8
00:02:18.514 --> 00:02:23.145
Now suppose your books posted the same deposit twice: a double credit.

9
00:02:25.587 --> 00:02:34.293
Continuity fails it and says why: more than one credit matches this deposit. That is money paid out twice, caught before it leaves.

10
00:02:34.301 --> 00:02:38.740
Export evidence saves the full report as a file for your records.
